  "diff": "--- review1-before\n+++ review1-successor\n@@ -47,4 +47,4 @@\n \n 4.1 A basis generator in the local chain group consists of a base singular simplex $\\sigma$ together with one point of the covering fiber over its first vertex and a scalar in $k$. Send it to the unique lift of $\\sigma$ with that first vertex, given in step 2.1, with the same scalar. Pairs of this kind are in bijection with covering singular simplices: projection gives the inverse map. Both directions are uniquely specified on generators, hence define inverse maps on all finite chains without selecting a lift for each base simplex. For the face omitting the first vertex, the first point of the lifted face is the transport of the old first point along that edge, exactly the local coefficient boundary rule. The other faces keep the first point. Thus the bijection commutes with all signed boundary terms. It commutes with the right deck action on generators too, so gives the claimed actual chain isomorphism of right $k[G]$-module complexes. [step 2.1, step 3.1, construct, algebra]\n \n-5.1 Apply steps 1.2\u20136.1 to $Y=W_N$ and combine with steps 2.1 and 4.1. A based loop $u\\in N$ lifts to the based loop whose projection is $u$, so its coefficient-one class maps precisely to $[u]\\otimes1$. For the right action, move the constant-path base lift by $g=[a]$. The lift of $a^{-1}$ joins the old base lift to that new one. Rebase the translated lift of $u$ by this path and its inverse; its projection is $a^{-1}ua$. Connector contributions cancel in first homology by step 1.2, so this gives the stated conjugation action on $N_{\\mathrm{ab}}\\otimes k$. Different representatives of $g$ change it by an inner automorphism of $N$, trivial on $N_{\\mathrm{ab}}$. The zero criterion is now exactly the displayed isomorphism and its coefficient-one formula; for $N=P^s\\pi$ this is the defining next mixed-series kernel. For a based continuous map $f$ carrying $N$ into $N\\prime$, the covering map is explicitly $[\\gamma]\\mapsto[f\\circ\\gamma]$; the equivalence relation makes it well-defined. It commutes with endpoint projection, the induced quotient-group action and simplex lifting, so step 4.1 is natural for its coefficient maps. Steps 1.2\u20136.1 already prove naturality of the constant-coefficient identification, yielding the stated commutative homology comparison. All covering, chain and homology maps were explicit quotient maps or uniquely defined finite-lift maps, so the full coefficient and choice scope in the Statement is proved. [step 2.1, step 3.1, step 4.1, step 1.2, step 2.2, algebra] \u220e\n+5.1 Apply steps 1.2 and 2.2 to $Y=W_N$ and combine with steps 2.1 and 4.1. A based loop $u\\in N$ lifts to the based loop whose projection is $u$, so its coefficient-one class maps precisely to $[u]\\otimes1$. For the right action, move the constant-path base lift by $g=[a]$. The lift of $a^{-1}$ joins the old base lift to that new one. Rebase the translated lift of $u$ by this path and its inverse; its projection is $a^{-1}ua$. Connector contributions cancel in first homology by step 1.2, so this gives the stated conjugation action on $N_{\\mathrm{ab}}\\otimes k$. Different representatives of $g$ change it by an inner automorphism of $N$, trivial on $N_{\\mathrm{ab}}$. The zero criterion is now exactly the displayed isomorphism and its coefficient-one formula; for $N=P^s\\pi$ this is the defining next mixed-series kernel. For a based continuous map $f$ carrying $N$ into $N\\prime$, the covering map is explicitly $[\\gamma]\\mapsto[f\\circ\\gamma]$; the equivalence relation makes it well-defined. It commutes with endpoint projection, the induced quotient-group action and simplex lifting, so step 4.1 is natural for its coefficient maps. Steps 1.2 and 2.2 already prove naturality of the constant-coefficient identification, yielding the stated commutative homology comparison. All covering, chain and homology maps were explicit quotient maps or uniquely defined finite-lift maps, so the full coefficient and choice scope in the Statement is proved. [step 2.1, step 3.1, step 4.1, step 1.2, step 2.2, algebra] \u220e\n",
